  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nWednesday-Thursday:\nAnother hot and humid day expected on Wednesday before a pattern\nchange unfolds over the region. A mild start in the morning will\ngave way to highs reaching back into the upper 90s to near 100\nby Wednesday afternoon. This occurs ahead of an approaching\nshortwave and associated cold front, progged to bring beneficial\nrainfall to the area through mid-week. Ahead of the front, \ndewpoints reach into the upper 60s to lower 70s as low-level \nmoisture funnels in from the south. This will further be \nsupported by PWATs of 1.9 to 2.1 inches along the front. The \nstrong warm air advection may support scattered showers and \nthunderstorms ahead of the front by Wednesday afternoon, though \nthere is uncertainty with the extent of coverage as the main \nforcing still remains displaced further to the west. With this \nin mind, isolated convection and cloud cover may keep parts of \nthe area cooler through the afternoon, but currently does not \noutweigh our confidence that heat remains a headline. Given the \ncurrent forecast of isolated convection and scattered cloud \ncover, heat index values are once again progged to reach 100 to \n105+. Given the longevity of this heat over a multiple day \nperiod and confidence in further heat impacts (NWS HeatRisk - \nMajor 3 of 4), we have elected to continue/extend a Heat \nAdvisory for much of the area on Wednesday. This only excludes \nportions of central MO at this time, where confidence is lower \nin extreme heat.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances (50-80%) increase across the \nnorthern part of the area through the late afternoon and evening\nas front approaches, becoming more widespread (60-90% chances) \nfurther south into Wednesday night and Thursday. Ample\ninstability (MUCAPE 2500-3000 J/kg) is forecast in the vicinity\nof marginal shear (0-6km 25-30 knots). Additionally, steeper low\nand mid level lapse rates should support a few stronger updrafts\nin this environment. As a result, the ingredients are in place\nfor a few isolated strong to severe thunderstorms. SPC depicts\nthis potential with a Marginal (1 of 5) Severe Outlook over the\narea on Wednesday into Thursday morning. Primary hazard with the\nstrongest thunderstorms will be damaging wind gusts of 50 to 60\nmph, though a vigorous updraft or two may support hail to the\nsize of quarters. Damaging winds may be further supported by\nprecipitable loading and rapid collapse of updrafts. Overall,\nwidespread severe weather is not expected.\n\nAs convection continues into Wednesday night and Thursday, there\nmay become additional focus on the training of activity over the\nsame areas. While this rainfall is beneficial to ongoing \ndrought conditions, too much rain in a short period of time can \nstill support isolated instances of flash flooding. For now, \nconfidence remains low in this potential given the groundwater \ndeficiency and departures from normal. Most locations receive \naround 0.50 to 1.0 inch of rainfall through Thursday, with \nlocalized corridors of 2 to 4 inches. Pinning down these exact \ncorridors will be best analyzed in the near term with mesoscale \ntrends. In general, this rainfall will remain beneficial to \nongoing drought conditions and increasing fire weather danger.\n\nFriday-Monday:\nAdditional disturbances look to move through the area on a daily\nbasis into and through the weekend. This features a rather\npessimistic rain chance forecast (20-50%) as we will have to pin\ndown each daily disturbance. Expect additional PoP\nchanges/modifications with future updates. NBM temp spreads have\nshrunk some which leads to higher confidence that temps will\nremain closer to average in the 80s to near 90.",
